<title>Exercise</title>
<category></category>
<!--
<description></description>
<img src="pix/cards/xxx.png" alt=""/>
-->
<body>

<p>I was thinking about the stars on your calendar for exercise, and about why
I exercise. I know exercising can seem like a big chore and a "should", but it
doesn't feel that way to me.</p>

<p>I have a lot of things I do during the day that require effort, and in
theory have some positive outcome. A lot of those things are difficult, because
the outcome is vague, or hard to see, or ambiguous. They require aggravating
effort that isn't enjoyable. Sometimes the outcome doesn't come until after
many weeks. Sometimes the outcome only happens if other people do what they are
supposed to do, which I can't always control, or requires a whole new kind of
effort. Sometimes the effort means doing things I don't understand and would
rather not learn how to do. And most of these things I do because I need to or
I'm supposed to, or because someone else told me to.</p>

<p>Exercising is one of the things I do just because *I* want to. The effort
has a long-term positive outcome (fitness, life expectancy, whatever), but what
really gets me to do it is the short-term outcome: I like the feel of muscles
that have done their physical work. I like the calmness I feel after showering
and relaxing. Some days it seems like I don't have enough time to do anything
physical, but then, if I insist on taking the time, it's a way I can declare a
personal victory over the other demands in my day.</p>

<p>I like feeling strong. I like feeling skilled at something physical. I like
getting away from screens and brain-work.</p>

<p>Exercise feels like the most direct effort I make, just for me, and not for
anyone else's benefit. It pays off.</p>

</body>
